Summary of Changes:
- CRASH ROOT CAUSE: Boss::update ends the defeat sequence with destroy(). The
  prune in PlayingState::update then frees the entity. updateBossArena(), ninety
  lines later in the SAME function, does `m_activeBoss->isActive()`. The pointer
  was cleared there, which is one frame too late — the free already happened.
  Added forgetEntity(), called from inside the prune before the unique_ptr is
  released, and it drops every observer pointer (boss, held shell, and m_player2,
  which the prune had never excluded so a dead P2 dangled too).
  LESSON: a raw observer pointer into an owning container must be cleared by the
  code that does the removal, not by whoever happens to read it next.
- SHELL PICKUP: PhysicsEngine::update cleared the movement intent flags in the
  MIDDLE of update(), before the collision resolution at the END of the same
  update() read them. isRunRequested() was therefore always false by the time
  the resolver asked, so "hold run to carry" could never fire in the real game.
  My own regression test passed because it called the resolver directly with the
  flag freshly set — the same shape of mistake as the old fireball test. Fixed by
  clearing the flags at the end of update().
  LESSON: when a test drives one function directly, it is not testing the order
  the game calls things in. Drive the pipeline.
- PIRANHA PLANT (reported as "the thwomper"): "Retracted" positioned the plant at
  its anchor, i.e. sitting on the pipe mouth in full view. Now drawn clipped to
  the portion above the mouth, so it slides out, and non-collidable while inside.
- "MADNESS OF MUSIC": Pipe::checkWarp was declared const and played a sound,
  teleported the player and published an event. PlayingState called it once per
  pipe per frame, so holding crouch re-fired everything every frame. And the
  event it published was CheckpointActivated ("Warp event type mapping" in the
  comment) — so every warp also set a respawn point, ran an auto-save and played
  the checkpoint jingle. Two overlapping sounds a frame at 60Hz. checkWarp is a
  pure query now; added a real PlayerWarped event; added a 0.75s cooldown; and
  the level swap moved out of the loop over m_entities that it was replacing.
- "VOID BUT NOT DYING": three gaps. The void check only tested Y, so a player
  past the map edge never fell and never died. A pipe exit was trusted even when
  outside its destination. And a spawn point in the void respawned you into it
  repeatedly. All three closed, with a fallback to the level's own spawn point.
- MISSING SPRITES: four entity types drew NOTHING — not the placeholder.
  setupAnimations() sets m_hasAnimation whether or not the frames it names exist,
  and drawSprite() returns early on a zero-size sprite, so a wrong frame name is
  completely silent. Moving platform asked for "platform_medium", falling
  platform for "falling_platform_medium", conveyor for "platform_long"; the
  atlas has none of them. Rather than fix the reported one, added
  Entity::hasArtwork()/artworkSize() and a suite case that asks all forty factory
  types what they would actually draw. That found the other three and Pipe (a
  false positive — it draws via PipeRenderer, now reports honestly).
  METHOD NOTE: the first version of the sweep only checked m_hasAnimation and
  reported 0/40 clean. The flag is set unconditionally, so it proved nothing.
  Checking the resolved sprite's size is what found the real four.
- Peach's float and the pipe's crouch check named physical keys rather than the
  bound action, so rebinding silently broke both. Both use isActionHeld() now.
